I had a mini heart-attack moment while working on my Cisco 2921 router recently. Out of nowhere, my configurations completely vanished from the running state, and the router dumped me straight into the initial setup dialog.

Since I had already spent time configuring DHCP, SSH, usernames, and a bunch of other settings, I wanted to figure out what actually happened before panicking and rebuilding everything from scratch.

Step 1: Checking if the Config is Actually Gone
The very first thing I did was check whether my saved config was still sitting in NVRAM.

show startup-config

Sure enough, my hostname, DHCP pools, DNS servers, and usernames were all sitting right there.

This told me the configuration hadn't been erased at all. It was safely stored on the router; for some reason, the box just wasn't loading it when it booted up.

Step 2: Looking at the Boot History
Next, I pulled up the system info to see what the router had been up to.

show version

Buried in the output, I spotted this:

Router uptime is 1 hour, 55 minutes
System returned to ROM by power-on
Last reload reason: power-on

I hadn't intentionally restarted it, but the logs showed it had recently powered on. That explained the reset, but I still needed to find out why it ignored my saved settings.

Step 3: Finding the Culprit
I checked the configuration register to see how the router was instructed to boot:

show version | include register

And there it was:

Configuration register is 0x2142

Mystery solved. The configuration register controls how the router handles its boot sequence. When it's set to 0x2142, the router deliberately bypasses the startup configuration (which is the classic trick used for password recovery). That's why it was acting like a fresh, unconfigured box even though my real config was still safe in NVRAM.

To this moment, I'm not entirely sure what flipped that register in the first place, but at least I knew the cause.

Step 4: Fixing the Configuration Register
To get things back to normal, I hopped into global configuration mode and switched the register back to its default production value (0x2102).

configure terminal
config-register 0x2102
end

I ran a quick check to make sure the change registered:

show version | include register

The router output confirmed it:

Configuration register is 0x2142 (will be 0x2102 at next reload)

A quick note: I made sure not to save the current running configuration (write memory or copy running-config startup-config) at this point, because I didn't want to accidentally overwrite the good saved config I was trying to recover.

Where It Stands
The root cause is fixed and the register is updated. The final step is just doing a clean reload to watch it pull the original configuration normally.

What I Took Away From This
Don't panic immediately. Just because a router acts like it's wiped doesn't mean your config is actually gone. Always check show startup-config first.

Know your config registers. Understanding what 0x2142 and 0x2102 do is a lifesaver. It turns a potential hours-long rebuild into a two-minute fix.

It's usually the boot process, not the features. The issue had nothing to do with my DHCP or SSH settings—it was purely about how the router decided to wake up.
